<Project Sdk="Azure.Functions.Sdk">

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Not for HTTP: the app has no HTTP functions and no ASP.NET Core integration. Microsoft's
      isolated-worker guide recommends this reference for every .NET app as a cold-start
      optimisation ("Performance optimizations"), and it supplies Microsoft.Extensions.* from the
      shared framework, which restore then prunes from the package graph and the SBOM.
    -->
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
    <!--
      Referenced directly to hold it where it was. The worker's own core and gRPC assemblies, the
      Event Hubs SDK and Application Insights all load Azure.Core, and until Microsoft.Extensions.Azure
      was removed (unused: no assembly in the worker references it) its 1.14.1 was what lifted
      Azure.Core to 1.61.0. Without this, restore resolves 1.49.0. Raising it is a change of its own:
      1.62.0 and 1.63.0 list breaking changes in the credential types.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Azure.Core" Version="1.63.0" />
    <!--
      Referenced directly to lift it above the 5.11.5 that the Event Hubs worker extension pulls
      in. On 5.11.5, EventData.EnqueuedTime casts the enqueued-time annotation to DateTimeOffset,
      but the worker rebuilds each event from AMQP bytes, where that annotation is a DateTime, so
      every invocation threw InvalidCastException. 5.12.2 reads either type. TelemetryFunctionTests
      guards this. Can go once the extension itself depends on 5.12.2 or later.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Azure.Messaging.EventHubs" Version="5.12.2" />
    <!--
      The credential for the dead-letter queue client (ADR-010): a ManagedIdentityCredential for the
      Function's user-assigned identity, built from the same DeadLetterStorage__credential and
      __clientId settings the queue trigger reads. Application Insights already brings 1.17.0 into
      the graph; referenced directly because StorageDeadLetterQueue uses it, so that it is not held
      wherever a dependency happens to leave it.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Azure.Identity" Version="1.21.0" />
    <!--
      The client ProcessTelemetry writes dead-letter entries with, and RetryForwarding moves an entry
      to the poison queue with (ADR-010). Not an output binding: a write that fails inside the
      function can be caught and logged with the message it concerns, which a binding the host
      writes after the function has returned cannot. The queue extension below brings 12.21.0;
      referenced directly because the code uses it.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Azure.Storage.Queues" Version="12.28.1" />
    <!--
      Held on 2.x deliberately. Application Insights 3.x is a breaking rewrite that removed types,
      such as ITelemetryInitializer, which Microsoft.Azure.Functions.Worker.ApplicationInsights
      (built against the 2.x line) still uses. A 3.x package compiles, then crashes the worker on
      startup with a TypeLoadException. WorkerCompositionTests guards this; dependabot.yml ignores
      major updates until the Functions integration supports 3.x.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.ApplicationInsights.WorkerService" Version="2.23.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Azure.Functions.Worker" Version="2.52.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Azure.Functions.Worker.ApplicationInsights" Version="2.51.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Azure.Functions.Worker.Extensions.EventHubs" Version="6.5.0" />
    <!--
      The queue trigger of RetryForwarding (ADR-010). Like the Event Hubs extension, it also adds a
      host extension to the project Azure.Functions.Sdk generates under obj/azure_functions/, which
      no SBOM and no Dependabot run sees (https://github.com/asnielsen789/IIoT-platform/issues/35).
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Azure.Functions.Worker.Extensions.Storage.Queues" Version="5.5.5" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience" Version="10.11.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\IIoT.Core\IIoT.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
